<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis the Pious</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/778">778</a>–<a href="/years/840">840 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis the Pious was the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, ruling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> from 814 to 840. His attempts to arrange the succession among his sons provoked rebellions and civil wars. After his death the empire was divided among his sons by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in 843.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious.jpg" alt="Louis the Pious" width="318" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ludwik_I_Pobo%C5%BCny.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis was born in 778, the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and Hildegard, while his father was campaigning in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>. He was crowned King of Aquitaine as a three-year-old child in 781 and sent there with regents and a court, since Charlemagne wished his sons to be raised in the lands they would rule. As king he was charged with defending the empire's southwestern frontier. After a siege of seven months, his army took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba</a> in 801. His brothers Pepin and Charles died in 810 and 811, and he was crowned co-emperor with his father at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> on 11 September 813.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Louis inherited the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> on his father's death in 814. He began by purging the court of those he considered morally dissolute, sent his unmarried half-sisters to nunneries and made the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-of-aniane">Benedict of Aniane</a> the reformer of the Frankish church. In 817 he issued the decree known as the Ordinatio Imperii, which made his eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a> co-emperor and gave Aquitaine to Pepin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>, while declaring the empire undivided. It omitted his nephew Bernard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, who plotted against him, surrendered and was blinded, dying after two days. In 833 Louis did penance for this at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attigny">Attigny</a>.</p><p>The birth of his son Charles, by his second wife Judith, led to civil wars that marked the last two decades of his reign, since the older sons refused to accept the changes he made in Charles's favour. In 830 Pepin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> captured him, but he was freed in 831.</p><p>In 833, on the plain of the Rothfeld, most of his army deserted him in what was called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-of-lies">Field of Lies</a>. A synod deposed him in November 833 and made him do public penance, and he was restored in March 834. A third civil war began in 838 when Pepin died and Louis tried to give Aquitaine to Charles. On the frontiers he fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> sacked Utrecht and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in the 830s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Louis died on 20 June 840 on an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> near Ingelheim, in the arms of his half-brother Drogo. He pardoned his son Louis and proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a> emperor. Fighting between the brothers continued until 843, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> divided the empire into three sovereign realms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> became the kernels of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He was buried in the Abbey of Saint-Arnould in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>. His reign is generally compared unfavourably with his father's, although he faced different problems.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis the Pious” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Avars are last mentioned in 822, when their envoys appeared at an assembly of <b>Louis the Pious</b> at Frankfurt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Two of Charlemagne's three legitimate adult sons, Charles the Younger and Pepin of Italy, died before him, which allowed <b>Louis the Pious</b> to inherit the undivided empire in 814 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Charlemagne had created a sub-kingdom of Aquitaine for his son <b>Louis the Pious</b> in 781, but by the late ninth century real power there had passed to great regional nobles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Hamburg takes its name from the Hammaburg, a fortification built in the early ninth century near the meeting of the Alster and the Elbe, probably under Charlemagne or <b>Louis the Pious</b>, to secure the Frankish frontier in Saxony.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Royal Frankish Annals record “leaders of the Slavs who live along the Danube” at <b>Louis the Pious</b>'s court in 811 and embassies of Moravians at Frankfurt in 822, the earliest certain reference to them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Charlemagne and <b>Louis the Pious</b> ruled through assemblies, written ordinances called capitularies, travelling royal agents called missi dominici and the network of counts, and tried to bind the realm together through a common Christian discipline, set out in the Admonitio generalis of 789.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chasseneuil-du-poitou">Chasseneuil-du-Poitou</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingelheim-am-rhein">Ingelheim am Rhein</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.65,0.37&amp;year=818" aria-label="Chasseneuil-du-Poitou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4559 524 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louis the Pious"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5009 794h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5009 794h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.65,0.37&amp;year=818">What was here in 818 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
